<h3>4. Equality reasoning</a></h3>

<p>
To illustrate some issues when combining lemmas, we choose equality
reasoning. To simplify matters this example will concentrate on
untyped lambda-terms. They are defined as before but
lambda-abstractions do not carry type annotations. The example is a
quite simple, but it has the advantage that it is easily
understood and helps the novice understand the issues arising when
formalizing a given formal system together with proofs. We first
define a declarative definition of equality which we call
<codeinline>equal</codeinline> and then give an algorithmic equality
definition, called <codeinline>eq</codeinline> where general
reflexivity and transitivity rules can be proven to be
admissible. Then we prove the equivalence between both definitions of
equality. 
</p>
<p>First, we define the declarative equality using
<codeinline>equal</codeinline>. In particular, it has a generic
reflexivity rule, called <codeinline>e_r</codeinline>, and a generic
transitivity rule, called <codeinline>e_t</codeinline>. 
</p>
<pre class="code">
equal: exp &rarr; exp &rarr; type.
e_l: ({x:exp} equal x x &rarr; equal (T x) (T' x)) 
     &rarr; equal (lam (\x. T x)) (lam (\x. T' x)).
e_a: equal T2 S2  &rarr; equal T1 S1 
     &rarr; equal (app T1 T2) (app S1 S2).
e_r: equal T T.
e_t: equal T' S &rarr; equal T T' 
     &rarr; equal T S.
</pre>

<p>For each variable we add the fact that <codeinline>equal x x</codeinline>.  
  Next, we define the algorithmic equality using <codeinline>eq</codeinline>. 
</p>
<pre class="code">
eq: exp &rarr; exp &rarr; type.   
eq_app : eq E1 F1 &rarr; eq E2 F2 
         &rarr; eq (app E1 E2) (app F1 F2).

eq_lam :  ({x : exp} eq x x &rarr; eq (E x) (F x)) 
          &rarr; eq (lam (\x. E x)) (lam (\x. F x)).
</pre>

<p>For algorithmic equality, we can prove reflexivity to be
admissible. Before we state the reflexivity and transitivity theorems
we will carefully define the context used when reasoning about the
equality of terms.  
<p>

<p>
<table border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=1>
<TR>
<TD> &Psi; </TD><TD> ::= </TD> <TD> . | &Psi;, eq x x
</TR>
</table>
</p>

<p>We define the shape of contexts in Beluga using context schema as follows:

<pre class="code">
<keyword>schema</keyword> eqCtx = some [] block x: exp . eq x x ;
</pre>
</p>

<p>
<strong>Theorem (Reflexivity):</strong> For all <codeinline>M</codeinline>,  &Psi; &#x22A2; <codeinline>eq M M</codeinline>.
</p>

<p>The reflexivity theorem is represented as a computation-level type in Beluga and its proof corresponds to a recursive program of this type.
</p>

<pre class="code">
<keyword>rec</keyword> ref : {g:(eqCtx)*} {M::exp[g]} (eq (M &hellip;) (M &hellip;))[g] =
<keyword>&Lambda;</keyword> g &rArr; <keyword>mlam</keyword> M &rArr; <keyword>case</keyword> [g] (M &hellip;) <keyword>of</keyword>
| [g] #p.1 &hellip; &rArr; [g] #p.2 &hellip;              

| [g] lam \x. N &hellip; x &rArr;
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g,b:<keyword>block</keyword> y:exp. eq y y] E &hellip; b =  
    ref [g, b:<keyword>block</keyword> q:exp. eq q q] &lt;g, b. N &hellip; b.1&gt; <keyword>in</keyword>
    [g] eq_lam \x. \w. (E &hellip; &lt; x, w &gt;)  

| [g] app (M1 &hellip;) (M2 &hellip;)  &rArr;
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] E1 &hellip; = ref [g] &lt; g . M1 &hellip; &gt; <keyword>in</keyword>
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] E2 &hellip; = ref [g] &lt; g . M2 &hellip; &gt; <keyword>in</keyword>
    [g] eq_app (E1 &hellip;) (E2 &hellip;)
;
</pre>

<p>
<strong>Theorem (Transitivity):</strong> If &Psi; &#x22A2; <codeinline>eq M K</codeinline> and &Psi; &#x22A2; <codeinline>eq K N</codeinline> then &Psi; &#x22A2; <codeinline>eq M N</codeinline>.
</p>

<p>The transitivity theorem is represented as a computation-level type in Beluga and its proof corresponds to a recursive program of this type.
</p>

<pre class="code">
<keyword>rec</keyword> trans: {g:(eqCtx)*}(eq (M &hellip;) (K &hellip;))[g]  &rarr; (eq (K &hellip;) (N &hellip;))[g]    
        &rarr; (eq (M &hellip;) (N &hellip;))[g]  =
<keyword>&Lambda;</keyword> g &rArr; <keyword>fn</keyword> e1 &rArr; <keyword>fn</keyword> e2 &rArr; <keyword>case</keyword> e1 <keyword>of</keyword>
| [g] #p.2 &hellip; &rArr; e2

| [g] eq_lam \x.\u. D1 &hellip; x u &rArr;
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] eq_lam \x.\u. D2 &hellip; x u =  e2  <keyword>in</keyword>	
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g, b:block x:exp. eq x x] E &hellip; b = 
    trans [g, b:block x':exp. eq x' x'] 
        ([g, b] D1 &hellip; b.1 b.2)
        ([g, b] D2 &hellip; b.1 b.2)
  <keyword>in</keyword>
    [g] eq_lam \x. \u. E &hellip; &lt;x, u&gt;
       
| [g] eq_app (D1 &hellip;) (D2 &hellip;) &rArr;
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] eq_app (F1 &hellip;) (F2 &hellip;) = e2 <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] E1 &hellip; = trans [g]  ([g] D1 &hellip;)  ([g] F1 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword>
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] E2 &hellip; = trans [g]  ([g] D2 &hellip;)  ([g] F2 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword>
    [g] eq_app  (E1 &hellip;)  (E2 &hellip;)
;
</pre>

<p>
Finally, we will show the proof for completeness, i.e., every proof using declarative equality can be translated in a proof using algorithmic equality.</p>

<pre class="code">
<keyword>schema</keyword> equalCtx = <keyword>some</keyword> [] <keyword>block</keyword> x: exp, u:eq x x . equal x x ;

<keyword>rec</keyword> ceq: {g:(equalCtx)*} (equal (T &hellip;) (S &hellip;))[g] &rarr; (eq (T &hellip;) (S &hellip;))[g]  =
<keyword>&Lambda;</keyword> g &rArr;  <keyword>fn</keyword> e &rArr; <keyword>case</keyword> e <keyword>of</keyword> 
| [g] #p.3 &hellip; &rArr; [g] #p.2 &hellip;

| [g] e_r &rArr; ref [g] &lt; g. _ &gt;

| [g] e_t (D2 &hellip;) (D1 &hellip;) &rArr; 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] F2 &hellip; = ceq [g] ([g] D2 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] F1 &hellip; = ceq [g] ([g] D1 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
    trans [g] ([g] F1 &hellip;)  ([g] F2 &hellip;)           

| [g] e_l (\x.(\u. (D &hellip; x u))) &rArr; 
  <keyword>let</keyword> {F:: (eq (R &hellip; x) (Q &hellip; x))[g, x:exp, u: eq x x]}
     [g,b:block x:exp,u:eq x x . equal x x] F &hellip; b.1 b.2 = 
        ceq [g, b:block x:exp, u:eq x x . equal x x]   ([g, b] D &hellip; b.1 b.3)
  <keyword>in</keyword>
     [g] eq_lam (\x.\v. F &hellip; x v)

| [g] e_a (D2 &hellip;) (D1 &hellip;) &rArr; 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] F1 &hellip; = ceq [g] ([g] D1 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] F2 &hellip; = ceq [g] ([g] D2 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
      [g] eq_app (F1 &hellip;) (F2 &hellip;)

;


<keyword>rec</keyword> ceq_main: (equal T S)[ ] &rarr; (eq T S)[ ] =  <keyword>fn</keyword> e &rArr;  ceq [ ] e ;
</pre>

<p>
The most interesting part about this proof is the fact that we need context subsumption. For example, in the case where we have applied as a last rule to <codeinline>equal (T &hellip;) (S &hellip)</codeinline> the rule <codeinline>e_r</codeinline>,  we refer to the reflexivity lemma by calling the previously defined function <codeinline>ref</codeinline>. However, the function <codeinline>ref</codeinline> expects a contxt <codeinline>eqCtx</codeinline>. Since the context schema <codeinline>eqCtx</codeinline> is a prefix of the schema <codeinline>equalCtx</codeinline>, we can always weaken any object originally defined in a context of schema <codeinline>eqCtx</ctxinline> to obtain an object defined in a context of schema <codeinline>equlaCtx</codeinline>.
</p>


